# .gitattributes — makes this repo behave identically on Windows, macOS and Linux.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: this project is authored on Windows and will be reviewed by
# people who may clone it on a Mac. Without this file, what ends up in each
# checkout depends on how each person's git happens to be configured
# (core.autocrlf), which means the same commit can produce different files on
# different machines. That is the sort of thing that makes a reviewer's test run
# fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the code.
#
# text=auto  -> git stores every text file with Unix (LF) endings internally and
#               converts on checkout to whatever the platform expects.
# eol=lf     -> for these types, always hand out LF, even on Windows. Editors on
#               Windows have handled LF fine for years, and it keeps the working
#               copy byte-identical to what is stored.
#
# Consequence to know about: this is why `git add` printed
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" before this file
# existed. Those warnings stop now.

* text=auto

*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# Cached vendor pages are data, not source. Never transform them: the corpus
# records a sha256 of each page's content, and rewriting line endings would
# change that hash and make "replay detects the page changed" fire falsely.
data/cache/html/** -text

# Binary — never diff, never convert.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
